req PUT "${API}/api/v1/control-plane/tenants/${TENANT_ID}/brands/${BRAND_ID}/locations/${LOCATION_ID}/place" 200 \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ${ADMIN_TOKEN}" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -H "Idempotency-Key: $(idemp)" \
  -d '{"addressLine":"12 Amir Temur Street","district":"Yunusabad","city":"Tashkent","contactPhone":"+998712000001","latitude":41.3350,"longitude":69.2880,"coordinateSource":"MERCHANT_PIN"}' \
  > /dev/null
evidence "location place recorded (Tashkent, 41.3350,69.2880)"

# GAP (application code — confirmed live, worked around by having
# platform-admin do this instead of the tenant owner; named as Gap C in the
# runbook, NOT fixed in code because the fix is a real design decision, not a
# one-line change): TenantAccessPolicy.requireTenantManagement, which gates
# createBrand/createLocation/describeLocation, accepts either the global
# platform-admin realm role OR an org-nested Keycloak client role
# ("tenant-owner"/"tenant-admin") read from the token's own
# "organization"."<org>"."resource_access" claim. Nothing in this codebase —
# not TenantOwnerLinkOrInvite, not any script — ever assigns that nested
# role; OrganizationProvisioner's own class javadoc explains why
# (ensureOrganizationRoles was deliberately left unimplemented: Keycloak 26.7's
# Organizations Admin REST API has no organization-scoped role sub-resource).
# TenantOwnerAuthorityGrantorAdapter instead confers the tenant-owner
# capability BUNDLE through iam.grants — which is real and sufficient for
# every @RequiresCapability-gated endpoint (legal entity, catalog, payments,
# channels — everything phase 3 below does) — but requireTenantManagement is
# a second, independent, older check that iam.grants cannot satisfy. A
# linked tenant owner is therefore capability-eligible but access-policy-
# blocked from ever creating a second brand or location for their own
# tenant. Verified live in this same script below, right after the owner
# signs in, as a deliberate negative check — then this run proceeds with
# platform-admin owning brand/location creation, which is what a real
# deployment would do too until this gap is closed.
gap "TenantAccessPolicy.requireTenantManagement will refuse the tenant owner on createBrand/createLocation later in this run (confirmed live below) — no code path ever grants the Keycloak org-nested role it checks. See docs/runbooks/proving-run.md Gap C."

